WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:07.820
So we're measuring it. Does that mean it changes at different points on the Earth or in time? What do you mean? Isn't gravity the same all the time?

2
00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:14.480
Great, great question. So to a certain approximation, gravity pretty much is the same everywhere.

3
00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.020
In a high school physics course, you

4
00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.360
may learn about 9.8 meters per second per second

5
00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.560
is the acceleration of an object when you drop it.

6
00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:23.840
I guess in the old days, it was 32 feet per second per second.

7
00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:27.680
But it turns out that number changes a little bit

8
00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:29.680
as you move around the surface of the Earth.

9
00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:33.140
So the Earth itself isn't really a sphere.

10
00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:35.980
It's more of a squished sphere called an ellipsoid.

11
00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:39.180
So the Earth is wider the equator than it is from pole

12
00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:39.540
to pole.

13
00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:40.740
That's because the Earth is spinning,

14
00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:44.520
and it kind of bulges out due to its own acceleration.

15
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.240
So at the pole, you're actually closer to the center

16
00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:49.360
of the Earth than you are at the equator.

17
00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:51.580
And Newton's law would tell you that if you're

18
00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:52.700
closer to the center of the Earth,

19
00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:53.880
gravity will be stronger there.

20
00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:59.340
And sure enough, it's something like a half percent effect.

21
00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:01.740
Gravity gets half percent stronger

22
00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:03.200
if you go from the equator to the poles.
